; A MAP'S KEYS, ASCENDING (maps.md §7).
; inputs: 1 2 4
; expect: 20 1720 11141720
;
; The keys are inserted DESCENDING -- 20, 17, 14, 11 -- and must come back
; ascending on every target. That order is what the construct is FOR: a host's
; iteration order is not canonical. Go randomises on purpose, JavaScript
; specifies its own, Java leaves it unspecified, and windows has only hash-slot
; order. Sorting by the order on K is what makes the answer the same on four
; hosts, precisely because it ignores all four.
;
; The result is an `Array`, whose index set is `Fin n` and therefore ORDERED, so
; producing one from an unordered index set REQUIRES supplying an order -- and
; the only order available canonically is the one on K itself. Insertion order
; is the other candidate and the algebra rules it out: a map is a SET.
;
; Each answer packs the keys base 100, so a wrong order is a different number
; rather than the same digits rearranged.
(def run (fn (n)
  (let (build-map 8 (fn (m)
         (loop ((m m) (i 0))
           (>= i n)  m
           else       (again (insert m (- 20 (* 3 i)) i) (+ i 1)))))
    (fn (m)
      (let (keys m) (fn (ks)
        (loop ((acc 0) (j 0))
          (>= j (len ks))  acc
          else              (again (+ (* acc 100) (ks j)) (+ j 1)))))))))
